\subsection*{Part II — Ongoing Challenges}
\subsection{Registration Costs as Barrier to Entry}

Registering a subnet, miner UID, or validator hotkey requires a dynamic \TAO\ fee
that is burned upon payment—rising with demand. At peak activity, subnet
registration has exceeded 100 \TAO, equivalent to thousands of dollars at market
price. This creates a de facto economic barrier that contradicts the network's
permissionless ideals—favouring well-capitalised participants over talented but
under-resourced ones.~[7,14]

\subsection{Centralisation Through Coordinated Entry}

Nothing prevents a well-resourced organisation from entering a subnet as a coordinated
team—bringing superior compute, proprietary models, and institutional expertise
simultaneously. A frontier AI lab entering together could dominate validator scoring,
accumulate bonds before any competitor responds, and capture the majority of
emissions—recreating the centralised dominance the protocol was designed to prevent.\\[0.4cm]

As emission rewards grow, the incentive for large players to enter increases
proportionally. The network's long-term defence against this is the same as Linux
against proprietary software: not any single contributor, but the aggregate force
of an open, globally distributed, incentivised community.

\subsection{Opentensor Foundation Centralisation Risk}

Despite its decentralised architecture, Bittensor's protocol upgrades still depend on
the Opentensor Foundation—a centralised entity. The Foundation controls the
Triumvirate, which initiates all runtime changes. There is no technical guarantee
they follow through on their commitment to fully decentralise governance. Until the
Triumvirate is dissolved and on-chain governance is fully community-driven, a single
organisation retains the ability to alter the rules the entire network operates under.

\subsection{Network Capacity Limits}

Bittensor is currently capped at 128 subnets, each with a maximum of 256 UIDs—64
validators and 192 miners. As the network grows, these limits become structural
bottlenecks. While the cap is a deliberate design choice with plans to increase limits
in later halvings, it currently constrains the very open participation the protocol
promises.

\subsection{Incentive Mechanism Quality and the Oracle Problem}

Any participant can register a subnet and design its incentive mechanism. There is
no upfront quality filter—a poorly designed or trivially gameable scoring function
can reward the wrong behaviour entirely, wasting emissions on low-quality output.
This is compounded by the oracle problem: for complex tasks such as reasoning or
code generation, objectively scoring miner output is genuinely difficult. The market
eventually punishes bad subnet design—but not before emissions are wasted.





