\section{Subnets}
\label{chap:subnets}

\subsection{What is a Subnet?}

A Subnet is a \textbf{specialised, self-contained competitive market} built on
top of the Bittensor protocol, dedicated to a specific computational task or
digital commodity~[10]. If Bittensor is the global brain, a subnet is a specific
functional lobe—purpose-built, independently governed, and ruthlessly competitive.\\[0.4cm]

Each subnet defines its own problem, its own metric of success, and its own
incentive mechanism. Miners compete to solve the problem. Validators judge the
quality of solutions. Emissions reward the winners. There is no fixed salary,
no guaranteed participation, and no protection from being outperformed~[7,10].\\[0.4cm]

This architecture deliberately mirrors \textbf{Darwinian natural selection}.
Every tempo, every miner's output is evaluated and scored. Those who perform
best capture the largest share of emissions. Those who fall behind receive less
and less until participation becomes economically unviable. There is no tenure,
no loyalty reward, and no institutional protection. The subnet does not care
who you are—only what you produce. Models that do not improve are replaced by
models that do. Subnets that do not attract capital and talent stagnate and
die~[10,26].\\[0.4cm]



This is not a metaphor. It is the \textbf{literal economic structure} of the
network: continuous selection pressure, applied every twelve seconds, across
every subnet simultaneously. The result is a system that grows more capable
over time not because anyone planned it to, but because the incentive structure
makes improvement the only rational long-term strategy~[3,8].\\


\noindent\textbf{Note:} There are 256 participants Max per Subnet (Currently). Per subnet, by default:\\
Total UIDs: 256 (all participants combined)\\
Validators: up to 64 slots (top 64 by emissions get validator permits)\\
Miners: up to 192 slots (remaining 256 - 64).\\
In practice most subnets have only 5--15 active validators — the 64 is just the maximum
ceiling. Unused validator slots are automatically available to miners. The subnet owner
can change these via the \texttt{max\_validators} hyperparameter.\\[0.4cm]


\subsection{How Subnets Should Work}

A well-functioning subnet follows a clear operational logic~[10]. The subnet owner
defines a \textbf{precise, measurable task}—one where good and bad outputs can
be objectively distinguished. Validators query miners, score their outputs
according to the owner's incentive mechanism, and submit weights to the
blockchain. Yuma Consensus aggregates those weights into consensus scores, and
emissions flow to miners proportionally~[9,11].\\[0.4cm]

The quality of a subnet is almost entirely determined by the quality of its
\textbf{incentive mechanism}~[14]. A well-designed mechanism creates a virtuous
cycle: clear evaluation criteria attract serious miners, serious miners produce
high-quality outputs, high-quality outputs attract stakers, stakers increase
the subnet's emission share, and higher emissions attract even better miners.
Conversely, a poorly designed mechanism—one with ambiguous scoring, gameable
metrics, or misaligned rewards—produces a race to the bottom.\\[0.4cm]

The subnet owner's 18\% emission share is therefore not passive income. It is
a \textbf{continuous incentive to maintain and improve the mechanism}~[11,16]. An owner
who neglects their subnet watches its Alpha token price fall, its emission share
shrink, and its best miners migrate to more rewarding subnets.

\subsection{Subnet Lifecycle}

\textbf{Registration.} Any participant can register a new subnet by paying a
registration fee denominated in \TAO. This fee is burned, reducing circulating
supply. As demand for subnet slots grows, the registration cost increases
dynamically—ensuring that only subnets with genuine economic conviction enter
the network~[7,10].\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{Bootstrapping.} A newly registered subnet begins with zero miners,
zero validators, and zero staked capital. The subnet owner must attract
participants by designing a compelling incentive mechanism and demonstrating
the utility of the task. Early emissions are low, but so is competition—
creating an opportunity window for early miners to accumulate bonds and
establish dominance~[10].\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{Growth.} As the subnet proves its utility, stakers allocate \TAO\
to its Alpha pool. Its emission share grows. More miners and validators
register. Competition intensifies. The quality of outputs improves. The
Alpha token price rises as a market signal of the subnet's value to the
broader network~[12,19].\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{Maturity.} A mature subnet operates as a stable, competitive market
with established validators, a diverse miner set, and consistent output
quality. Its Alpha token has undergone at least one halving, signalling that
a significant portion of its supply has been distributed to productive
participants~[16,26].\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{Death.} The network is capped at 128 subnets (Currently). When a new subnet
attempts to register and the cap is reached, the protocol automatically identifies the
weakest existing subnet using its EMA (Exponential Moving Average) price:

\[
  \text{EMA}(t) = \alpha \times P^{(t)} + (1 - \alpha) \times \text{EMA}^{(t-1)}
\]

The subnet with the lowest EMA price outside its immunity period is deregistered.
New subnets receive a 4-month immunity period during which they cannot be deregistered.
Upon deregistration, all Alpha tokens in the subnet's pool are liquidated and the
underlying \TAO\ is distributed proportionally to Alpha holders. The subnet owner
receives their registration lock cost back, minus the emissions they already received.
The market has rendered its verdict—automatically, mathematically, and without appeal.\\[0.4cm]

\subsection{Notable Subnets}

\subsection*{Subnet 62 — Ridges (Autonomous Software Engineering)}
Developed by \textbf{Ridges AI}, Subnet 62 tackles one of the most complex
problems in the digital economy: \textbf{autonomous software engineering}.
Miners deploy fully autonomous coding agents that compete to write, test,
and debug real software in response to engineering challenges—evaluated
purely on output quality. Unlike proprietary code assistants such as GitHub
Copilot or Cursor, Ridges is an open, permissionless meritocracy: the best
autonomous engineer wins, regardless of who built it or where in the world
they are. Ridges is built directly on top of Chutes (Subnet 64), using its
decentralised compute layer as infrastructure—demonstrating how subnets can
compose and build upon one another, forming a layered ecosystem of
specialised intelligence~[26].

\subsection*{Subnet 64 — Chutes (Decentralized AI Compute)}
Developed by \textbf{Rayon Labs}, Chutes is the \textbf{largest subnet on
Bittensor by market capitalisation} and one of the clearest demonstrations
of product-market fit in the entire ecosystem~[20,21]. It operates as a decentralised,
serverless AI compute platform—allowing developers to deploy and run any
open-source AI model via a standard API in seconds, with no infrastructure
management required.\\[0.4cm]

Chutes is a direct and economically superior alternative to centralised cloud
AI providers, delivering inference at approximately \textbf{85\% lower cost
than AWS} for comparable tasks~[21]. As of 2025, Chutes ranked as the
\textbf{number one inference provider by usage on OpenRouter}—the world's
most widely used AI model aggregation platform—placing it ahead of all
centralised providers including Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google~[20,22]. Miners are
GPU owners who earn \TAO\ for serving models reliably and efficiently;
the better their performance, the greater their emission share.


\subsection*{Subnet 44 — Score (Decentralized Sports Intelligence)}
\textbf{Score} is a decentralised computer vision platform built on Bittensor,
dedicated to collecting, annotating, and analysing football data at scale~[26].
Miners receive live video streams of football matches and compete to track
player movements, ball positions, and game events in real time using object
detection and keypoint analysis—producing structured, machine-readable
match data evaluated by validators for accuracy and speed.\\[0.4cm]

The problem Score solves is a real one: a single professional football match
currently requires hundreds of hours of manual annotation at a cost of
thousands of dollars. Score aims to reduce this by \textbf{10x to 100x}
while dramatically improving speed and accuracy—making the kind of
high-quality analytics previously reserved for elite clubs accessible to
any team, scout, or analyst in the world. With partnerships spanning
\textbf{283 leagues and 400,000 matches} of footage, and a fantasy sports
application planned for the \textbf{2026 World Cup}, Score demonstrates
that Bittensor's incentive architecture is not limited to language
models—it can be applied to any domain where quality, speed, and scale
of data processing matters~[26].

\subsection{Configurable Subnet Variables}

One of Bittensor's most powerful design features is that subnet owners are
not locked into a fixed architecture~[7]. A range of \textbf{hyperparameters}
can be adjusted by the subnet owner to tune the competitive dynamics of their
subnet~[10]:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.8}
\begin{tabular}{p{4.5cm} p{8.5cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Description} \\
\midrule
\texttt{tempo} & Number of blocks per scoring round. Controls how frequently
emissions are distributed and weights are updated. \\
\texttt{max\_n} & Maximum number of miners permitted in the subnet. Controls
competition density. \\
\texttt{min\_allowed\_weights} & Minimum weight a validator must assign.
Prevents validators from zeroing out miners entirely. \\
\texttt{max\_weight\_limit} & Maximum weight a validator can assign to a
single miner. Prevents winner-takes-all concentration. \\
\texttt{immunity\_period} & Number of blocks a new miner is protected from
deregistration. Gives new entrants time to establish performance. \\
\texttt{bonds\_moving\_avg} & The decay rate $\alpha$ in the bond update
formula. Controls how quickly validators build or lose bonds in miners. \\
\texttt{owner\_cut} & The subnet owner's share of emissions. Protocol default
is 18\%. \\
\texttt{validator\_permit\_threshold} & Minimum stake required to act as a
validator with scoring weight. Default is 1,000 \TAO. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Configurable subnet hyperparameters.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\end{table}

\noindent These parameters give subnet owners precise control over the
competitive dynamics of their market—balancing openness to new entrants,
protection against gaming, and the speed at which the network adapts to
new information. Getting these parameters right is as much an art as a
science, and represents one of the most active areas of subnet research
and iteration in the ecosystem~[10,14].


\section{Governance}
\label{sec:governance}

Bittensor separates two distinct layers of operation that are often conflated: network
participation and protocol governance. Mining, validating, staking, and subnet
operation are all governed automatically by the protocol—by mathematics, consensus,
and market forces. No human decision is required for emissions to flow, miners to be
scored, or subnets to compete. The network runs itself.~[7],~[28] \\[0.4cm]

Protocol governance—changes to the underlying rules of the network itself—operates
through a separate bicameral legislature composed of two bodies:\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{The Triumvirate} consists of three members from the Opentensor Foundation.
It is responsible for drafting and submitting proposals for protocol changes. It cannot
act unilaterally—every proposal requires Senate approval before execution.\\[0.4cm]

\textbf{The Senate} is composed of up to the top 12 delegate hotkeys by stake weight.
Senators vote on proposals submitted by the Triumvirate. A proposal passes only when
it receives a majority of Senate approvals ($>50\%$), after which a Triumvirate member
executes it on-chain.\\[0.4cm]

This structure ensures that no single party can unilaterally change the protocol.
The foundation can propose but not execute without community approval. The Senate
can block but not propose. Both chambers must agree.\\[0.4cm]

This governance model is explicitly designed as a transitional architecture. As
the network matures, the goal is to phase out the Triumvirate entirely and transition
to fully community-driven governance. Governance, like everything else on Bittensor,
is on a trajectory toward full decentralisation.\\[0.4cm]

Governance controls changes to the \textbf{runtime}—the core logic encoded into the
Bittensor blockchain (Subtensor) that defines everything from emission schedules and
subnet registration costs to consensus parameters and hyperparameter bounds. A runtime
upgrade is the blockchain equivalent of changing the constitution.













