\subsection{Statistical Significance}
\textit{Using AbstractML's funcs:\\
getConfidenceInterval( )\\
testAgainstBaseline()\\
performCV()
}\\
\\
Across all models and both hyperparameter optimization strategies, test-set
accuracy and F1 were significantly greater than the random-guess baseline of
0.5 ($\alpha = 0.05$). For example, the Bayesian-optimized Logistic Regression
model achieved an accuracy of 0.9264 (95\% CI [0.9224, 0.9302], Wilson
interval) and an F1 score of 0.9261 (95\% CI [0.9222, 0.9301], bootstrap),
both significantly above baseline (binomial test, $p < 0.001$; bootstrap test,
$p = 0.001$). This pattern of significance held consistently across all
model--method combinations evaluated.



To assess stability without reusing the held-out test set, stratified 5-fold cross-validation was performed on \texttt{hc3\_Train} using \texttt{performCrossValidation()} from AbstractML, with hyperparameters fixed to the best random-search configuration per sklearn model. SetFit was excluded due to fine-tuning cost on CPU. Table~\ref{tab:cv-results} reports mean $\pm$ standard deviation across folds.

